          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            This page is about PCT theorems in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-reversal+symmetry"} time-reversal symmetry
            (warning: the order of the letters P, C and T varies, some authors use CPT, for example). The laws of nature as described by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            are believed to be invariant if one simultaneously reverses the arrow of time, conjugates all charges and reverses all chiral properties. PCT theorems try to make this belief precise by defining the appropriate operators and showing that certain expressions remain constant. Both the statements and the proofs depend on the framework for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            one uses.
          %p Being “invariant” means that every process that can be observerd in our universe can be observed identically in the “mirror” universe, that is there is no experiment in our universe that cannot be duplicated in the mirror universe.
          %p For some time physicists believed that subsets of the PCT symmetry are respected by nature, but today there are counterexamples known for every subset, for example:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+nuclear+force"} weak nuclear force
                violates parity symmetry.
            %li
              %p Charge symmetry would violate the observation that the universe consists mostly of matter and not of matter and antimatter.
            %li
              %p
                For CP symmetry violation see Wikipedia:
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CP_violation"} CP violation
            %li
              %p All known physical laws are time symmetric, but since PCT symmetry is believed to hold and CP symmetry does not hold, there has to be a process that breaks T symmetry. As of today there is no consenus about what that process may be (there are good reasons to believe that it has nothing to do with the second law of thermodynamics).
          %h2#abstract Abstract
          %p …
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#definition_in_the_wightman_approach Definition in the Wightman approach
          %p
            The PCT theorem for Wightman fields (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+axioms"} Wightman axioms
          %p This proof clarified the different conditions one has to impose, these are:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Covariance of the theory under the (connected part of the)
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+group"} Poincare group
            %li
              %p Positivity of the energy.
            %li
              %p
                There are only fields, which transform with respect to finite dimensional representations of the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+group"} Lorentz group
            %li
              %p Locality, which means that for spacelike distances the Bose fields commute with all other fields and the Fermi fields anticommute with each other.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+space"} Minkowski space
                has even dimensions.
            %li
              %p To every field in the theory appears its conjugate complex partner.
          %h3#definition_in_the_haagkastler_approach Definition in the Haag-Kastler approach
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒥
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{J})
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} Haag-Kastler net
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong PCT operator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta
              on the local net is an anti-linear
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphism
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℳ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O})
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B \in \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O})
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda \in \mathbb{C}
              we have the relations
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Θ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi Θ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi Θ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta(A B) = \Theta(A) \Theta(B)
                  ;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Θ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi λ
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mover
                          %mi λ
                          %mo ¯
                        %mi Θ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta(\lambda A) = \overline \lambda \Theta(A)
                  ;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Θ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi ℳ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi ℳ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta(\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O})) = \mathcal{M}(-\mathcal{O})
                  ;
            %p
              such that for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Λ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Λ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Lambda,a) \mapsto U(\Lambda, a)
              the given
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+group"} Poincare group
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℳ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
              we have
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Θ
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Λ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi A
                        %mo =
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Λ
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi Θ
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta U(\Lambda, a) A = U(\Lambda, -a) \Theta A
                  ;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Θ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta
                  transforms every
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charge+sector"} charge sector
                  into its conjugate sector.
          %p
            A
            %strong PCT theorem
            in this context is a theorem that states sufficient conditions such that a PCT operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Theta
            exists.
